            <ArticleTitle>Are acupuncture points tender? A blinded study of Spleen 6.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>149-55</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the methodology for investigating the tenderness of acupuncture points, with particular reference to the hypothesis that the point Spleen 6 (SP6) is more tender in women than in men.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Single-blind, observational study with volunteers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University of Exeter, U.K.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Student volunteers, 13 male and 13 female.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pressure thresholds at SP6, a control acupuncture point Stomach 36, and two nonacupuncture control sites in each leg were measured weekly for 4 weeks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No statistically significant differences were found between the pressure thresholds of SP6 in women and those in men.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The present study provides no strong evidence to support the hypothesis that the acupuncture point SP6 is more tender in women and in men. Recommendations for further investigations are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
